<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agaja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as King Agaja</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agaja was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> in the early eighteenth century who expanded his kingdom towards the coast, conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a> and Whydah. His conquests disrupted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> network managed by coastal tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. In response Oyo launched repeated cavalry invasions and eventually forced Dahomey to pay tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agaja.jpg" alt="Agaja" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ji-Elle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Embl%C3%A8me_du_roi_Agadja-Mus%C3%A9e_africain_de_Lyon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Akaba and above all <b>Agaja</b>, who came to the throne in 1708 or 1718 according to different chronologies, Dahomey turned from an inland state into a power on the coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>King Agaja</b>’s conquests of Allada and Ouidah upset the coastal trading order, and from 1726 onwards Oyo invaded Dahomey repeatedly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Houegbadja (c. 1645–1685) built the Royal Palaces of Abomey and made offerings to the spirits the king’s alone, and he, Akaba and <b>Agaja</b> laid down much of the succession and the administration.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dahomey">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Dahomey</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1724 <b>Agaja</b> of Dahomey conquered Allada, which was absorbed into his kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-allada">Kingdom of Allada</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1727 <b>Agaja</b> of the Kingdom of Dahomey invaded and destroyed Savi, and King Haffon fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-whydah">Kingdom of Whydah</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/benin">Benin</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.68,2.13&amp;year=1729" aria-label="Allada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4609 1914 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Agaja"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5059 2184h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5059 2184h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=6.68,2.13&amp;year=1729">What was here in 1729? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
